<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.Title}}</title>
<meta name="description" content="Every City of Livermore department as a bar, longest first, split by the fund groups that pay for it; open one for what it spends under each object heading, every fund together, and which funds pay for it, each figure linked to the Budget Book page it was read from.">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
{{template "theme-stamp"}}
<script type="application/json" id="department-config">{{.ConfigJSON}}</script>
</head>
<body>

<div class="wip-banner" role="status">⚠️ Work in progress — early preview, coverage is incomplete.</div>

{{template "topbar" .}}

<header class="page-head">
  <div>
    <h1>{{.Title}}</h1>
    <p class="lede">{{.Lede}}</p>
  </div>
</header>

<main id="department-main">

<form class="department-controls" id="department-controls">
  <label>Department
    <select id="department-select">
      {{- range .Departments}}
      <option value="{{.ID}}">{{.Label}}</option>
      {{- end}}
    </select>
  </label>
  <label>Budget column
    <select id="year-select">
      {{- range .Years}}
      <option value="{{.Path}}"{{if eq .Path $.Opens}} selected{{end}}>{{.Lede}}</option>
      {{- end}}
    </select>
  </label>
</form>

<section class="department-bars" aria-labelledby="bars-heading">
  <h2 id="bars-heading">Every department</h2>
  <p class="department-bars-how">
    Each bar is a department, longest first, on the one scale the longest sets,
    split by the group of funds that pays for it. Click a department, or press
    Enter or Space on it, to open its fund groups and to show its two blocks
    of the Budget Book below the bars, with the page each cell was read from.
  </p>
  <ul class="department-legend" id="department-legend" aria-label="Fund groups, largest across every department first"></ul>
  <ol class="department-list" id="department-list"></ol>
  <p class="department-bars-sum" id="department-bars-sum">
    ◇ A bar's figure is our sum of the department's funding rows in the column,
    not a total the city prints, and each share is ours too: a fund group's part
    of that sum, rounded to a tenth.
  </p>
</section>

<div class="department-view" id="department-view">

<h3 id="department-heading">Choose a department</h3>

{{- /* The two sentences the client chooses between, worded here. Whether a
       department's two blocks tie in a column is Go's declaration, carried
       per year in the config; the client only picks which to print. */}}
<p class="department-tie" id="department-tie"
   data-ties="Both blocks of this department's page add up to the same sum in this column, to the cent; fisc verify fails if they stop doing so."
   data-held="In this column the two blocks do not add up to the same sum, and the project's checks declare why: "></p>

<div class="department-sides">

<section class="department-side" aria-labelledby="funding-heading">
  <h4 id="funding-heading">Who pays for it</h4>
  <p id="funding-says">
    Read from the department's <strong>Department Funding Sources</strong> block,
    the lower half of its page in the Budget Book (pp.85-125): one row per fund.
    <strong>Each figure is one fund's share</strong> of the department, not its
    whole cost. The General Fund row is the General Fund's share alone; every other
    row is a fund the city keeps apart from it.
  </p>
  <p id="internal-service-says">
    A fund marked <em>internal service</em> is one the city fills by charging its own
    departments, so the money it pays here was first charged to another department
    and is counted in that department's cost as well.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="department-table" id="funding-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Fund</th><th scope="col">Fund group</th><th scope="col" class="num">Amount</th><th scope="col" class="num">Share ◇</th><th scope="col">Page</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="department-side" aria-labelledby="spending-heading">
  <h4 id="spending-heading">What it spends, every fund together</h4>
  <p id="spending-says">
    Read from the department's <strong>Expenditures by Category</strong> block, the
    upper half of the same page: what each of its divisions spends under each object
    heading, whatever fund pays. <strong>Each figure is the whole department's
    cost</strong> under that heading, summed over every division the page prints
    under the department.
  </p>
  <p id="transfers-says" hidden>
    <strong>Transfers Out</strong> is money moved to another of the city's own funds,
    not spent on anything. The page prints it inside the division's total, so it is
    inside this side's sum too.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="department-table" id="spending-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Object heading</th><th scope="col" class="num">Amount</th><th scope="col" class="num">Share ◇</th><th scope="col">Pages</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h5>By division, as printed</h5>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="department-table" id="division-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Division</th><th scope="col">Object heading</th><th scope="col" class="num">Amount</th><th scope="col">Page</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

</div>

</div>

<p class="subtle">
  Every amount links to the extracted text of the page it was read from. A row
  marked <em>our sum</em> adds the cited rows above it and is ours, not the city's:
  the page prints a department total of its own, which this site has not read as a
  figure, so neither sum is presented as the department's total. A share marked ◇
  is ours too: one row as a percentage of its side's sum.
</p>

{{- template "caveats-line" .CaveatsHere}}

</main>

<footer>
  {{template "sources" .}}
  <p class="subtle">
    Scope {{.Scope}}. Projection: {{.ProjectionBy}}. Packaged by {{.ExportedBy}}.
    The column on screen is drawn from <a id="department-data-path" href="{{.DataPath}}">{{.DataPath}}</a>,
    which holds both blocks.
  </p>
</footer>

<template id="department-bar"><li class="department-bar"><div class="department-row" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="department-name"></span><span class="department-track"></span><span class="department-value"></span></div><div class="department-detail" hidden><ul class="department-groups"></ul><a class="department-to-tables" href="#department-heading">Its two blocks, cell by cell, below the bars</a></div></li></template>
<template id="department-seg"><span class="department-seg"></span></template>
<template id="department-key"><li><span class="department-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="department-key-name"></span></li></template>
<template id="department-group"><li><span class="department-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="department-group-name"></span><span class="department-group-value"></span><span class="department-group-share"></span></li></template>

<!-- department.js runs nothing on import, so boot() is called here. -->
<script type="module">import { boot } from "./department.js"; boot();</script>
</body>
</html>
